Tools:

    -----
    "WHAT" does it mean to give a model the tools?

    You are giving the model/llm the ability to connect to external functions to be able to call the function.
    It allows then to get:
        1.  Deeper/Richer responses b/c we can extent the knowledge cause it can use a tool to retrieve something
            perhaps from DB or anything that we connect the model with.
        2.  Ability to carryout the actions within the application.
        3.  It enhances capabilities, like executing the python code, write a test case, book a ticket.

    BUT WAIT:
    So we are going to give an LLM the ability to run the code?

    How did that happen? b/c an LLM is like a data science model. A model which predict tokens. It output token.
    How they started running functions which are on my computer? The answers is the don't :)

    The actual way it works is..
    1. The code makes a call to LLM. Like I want to do 1, 2 and 3...
    2. Then LLM inform code that, in order to do these we have to run a tool.
    3. Then it's our code runs the tool and come back with the answer
    4. Then we again call the LLM 2nd time with that answer and say hey this is our conversation history
        a. I ask you a question -> ("role": "user")
        b. You asked question the tool -> ("role": "assistant")
        c. Here the answer for that question, and now please answer the question -> ("role": "user")
    5. And, based on this conversation history by taking all that into account, LLM responds back.

    It's just more messages in the conversation history, and that messages include "a request to call a tool".
    That's all there is... the LLM is till just generating tokens.

    But how does LLM knows to respond with these tokens says "I did like to call this tool".
    This is very simple the first prompt that you send to LLM.
    You said "Hey I want you to do these following things, by the way I have the ability to run some tools on your
    behalf. If you want to run this tool simple say run this tool. I will get back to you".

    That's all there is in your first prompt you explained what tools can be runed in a way LLM has already been
    trained. So it's ready to expect that format of tool calling. You do it by describing Json.

